    "body": "Hi, we\u2019re trying to reproduce the iPSC experiment from Fig.\u202f2\u2014training only on day\u202f0 and day\u202f3 cells, then interpolating day\u202f1 and day\u202f2\u2014but we\u2019re not getting the reported behavior.\n\nBecause no checkpoint for this setting was provided, we trained our own model from scratch. Below is exactly what we did:\n\n### Data processing\n\n1. Start from the iPSC dataset referenced in the paper (Zenodo record\u202f3625024).\n2. Filter cells to keep only day\u202f0\u2013day\u202f3 samples, remove low-quality cells (total counts \u2265\u202f1,000; 200\u202f\u2264\u202fgenes expressed\u202f\u2264\u202f8,000; mitochondrial fraction \u2264\u202f20%).\n3. Normalize per cell to 1\u202f\u00d7\u202f10\u2074 counts, log1p transform.\n4. Select 203 highly variable genes (as described in the paper) and force-include the marker genes mentioned around Fig.\u202f2 (NANOG, T, GATA6, POU5F1, HAND1, MKI67, ETV2, CLDN5, PECAM1, SOX17, COL4A1, DCN, ACTA2, PDGFRB, FOXA2, EOMES, MIXL1, COL5A1, CXCR4, NR2F2, GJA4, PGF, RGS16, ADAMTS18).\n5. Training data: random balanced sample of 1,200 cells from day\u202f0 and 1,200 cells from day\u202f3 (so only day\u202f0/3 go into training).\n6. Evaluation data: separate balanced sample of 600 cells per day across day\u202f0\u20133 (used purely for interpolation/evaluation).\n\n### Training configuration\n\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=2 python train_squidiff.py \\\n       --logger_path logs/ipsc_fig2_run \\\n       --resume_checkpoint checkpoints/ipsc_fig2_run \\\n       --data_path datasets/ipsc_fig2_train.h5ad \\\n       --gene_size 203 \\\n       --output_dim 203 \\\n       --num_layers 3 \\\n       --batch_size 64 \\\n       --microbatch -1 \\\n       --lr 1e-4 \\\n       --diffusion_steps 1000 \\\n       --lr_anneal_steps 100000 \\\n       --log_interval 200 \\\n       --save_interval 2000 \\\n       --use_drug_structure False\n\nTraining with loss \u2248\u202f0.03, and gradients looked stable.\n\n### Evaluation procedure\n\n- Encode the evaluation split (day\u202f0\u20133), treat Group\u202f0 as \u201cstart\u201d (day\u202f0) and Group\u202f3 as \u201ctarget\u201d (day\u202f3).\n- Compute \u0394z in the semantic latent space and interpolate at fractions corresponding to days\u202f1 and\u202f2.\n- Generate 1,000 cells per interpolated day and compare to the held-out real day\u202f1/2 cells:\n      - UMAP overlay (real vs generated)\n      - Mean-expression scatter\n      - Flattened Pearson/R\u00b2/MAE/RMSE.\n\n### Observed results\n\nFor both day\u202f1 and day\u202f2 comparisons we get:\n\n- Mean-level Pearson \u2248\u202f0.17, R\u00b2 \u2248\u202f\u20130.04\n- Flattened R\u00b2 \u2248\u202f\u20130.06, RMSE \u2248\u202f0.58, MAE \u2248\u202f0.30\n- UMAPs show almost no overlap between real and generated cells; the model behaves like a noisy baseline rather than capturing the day\u202f1/2 state.\n\n### Paper reference\n\nFig.\u202f2b\u2013c and Supplementary Fig.\u202f2 report that Squidiff, trained only on days\u202f0 and\u202f3, \u201caccurately predicts single-cell transcriptomics from day\u202f1 to day\u202f3,\u201d showing smooth differentiation trajectories. We aren\u2019t seeing that behavior\u2014the interpolated cells stay far from the real day\u202f1/2 distribution.\n\n### Questions\n\n1. Are we missing any preprocessing steps (e.g., scaling, gene ordering, semantic-encoder options) that were crucial in your Fig.\u202f2 notebook?\n2. Do the iPSC experiments use different architecture settings (gene_size/output_dim, num_layers, channels, EMA, etc.) or training tricks (e.g., curriculum, lower LR) that we should replicate?\n3. Is the new Figshare checkpoint (\u201cVO_diff_model.pt\u201d) intended for this iPSC interpolation, or is there a separate checkpoint for Fig.\u202f2 that we should request?\n4. Any guidance on diagnosing why our R\u00b2 stays negative despite following the published description?\n\nThanks in advance for pointing out what we\u2019re missing\u2014we\u2019d love to line up our replication with the paper\u2019s results.",
